From fd77f76567911af2e3d1fba5295a4c067abfcf5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 23:36:50 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(feature_flags): force-enable acp-beta for local builds
 (per-turn token count, terminal auth)

Zed grants the `acp-beta` feature flag from the server, to staff accounts only,
so a locally built Zed never receives it. `AcpBetaFeatureFlag` declares no
`enabled_for_all` override (crates/feature_flags/src/flags.rs:24-27) and the
trait default is `false` (crates/feature_flags/src/feature_flags.rs:130-132),
which makes the four lines below the only way to turn the flag on in a source
build.

WHAT THE FLAG BUYS TODAY (verified 2026-07-31 by reading the vendored pin
1ac840ab2f3ef68c832ad8b4c7789a8d7f5e04a2; story 008, sub-task 7.2)

1. Per-turn token count. crates/acp_thread/src/acp_thread.rs:3867-3874 ingests
   a prompt response's `usage` only `if cx.has_flag::<AcpBetaFeatureFlag>()`,
   and it is the sole writer of `TokenUsage::input_tokens` / `output_tokens`.
   Those feed `update_turn_tokens`
   (crates/agent_ui/src/conversation_view/thread_view.rs:1422-1429, driven by
   `AcpThreadEvent::TokenUsageUpdated` at
   crates/agent_ui/src/conversation_view.rs:1806-1812), which is what renders
   the in-progress and the finished turn token labels (thread_view.rs:7313-7321
   and :6822-6832). With the flag off those counters only ever accumulate 0 and
   both labels are suppressed by the `> TOKEN_THRESHOLD` (250) filter.

   Stated precisely, because this is easy to over-claim: the flag does NOT gate
   the context indicator as a whole. The `SessionUpdate::UsageUpdate` path
   (acp_thread.rs:2640-2650) is unflagged and keeps filling
   max_tokens/used_tokens/cost, so the context ring works either way. Nor does
   the flag deliver the input/output breakdown to us: the split ring and
   tooltip (thread_view.rs:4771 and :5749) additionally require
   `supports_split_token_display` -> `as_native_thread` (:1194-1198), a
   downcast to `NativeAgentConnection` that always fails for an external ACP
   agent. For an external agent the flag's visible effect is the per-turn token
   count, nothing more.

2. Stabilized terminal authentication. crates/agent_servers/src/acp.rs:1887
   matches the typed `acp::AuthMethod::Terminal` arm only
   `if cx.has_flag::<AcpBetaFeatureFlag>()`. With the flag off it falls through
   (:1908-1909) to `meta_terminal_auth_task` (:1543-1566), the
   pre-stabilization path, which returns `None` unless the auth method carries
   a legacy `_meta["terminal-auth"]` object. claude-agent-acp-plus still ships
   that object alongside the typed method (src/acp-agent.ts:1306-1314 and
   :1336-1352), so login does still work with the flag off today; what the flag
   changes is that Zed honours the stabilized shape. The day the agent drops
   that deprecated `_meta` block, a flagless Zed's login entry silently does
   nothing.

WHY THE ORIGINAL JUSTIFICATION LAPSED

The patch was written for story 002 to unlock ACP elicitation form/url support,
which the flag gated at the time. It no longer does:
`client_capabilities_for_agent` (crates/agent_servers/src/acp.rs:764-792)
advertises `.elicitation(ElicitationCapabilities::new().form(..).url(..))` at
:786-790 unconditionally, and the function does not even take a `cx`, so no
flag could gate it. Forcing the flag buys nothing for elicitation now.

WHY RE-JUSTIFY RATHER THAN RETIRE

Upstream recycles this flag on purpose: "We reuse this feature flag for new
betas, so don't delete it if it is not currently in use"
(crates/feature_flags/src/flags.rs:19-21). Its call sites therefore change
meaning from release to release, which is precisely why "the patch still
applies" has never answered "the patch is still needed". Expect to re-justify
this header at pin bumps, not to trust it indefinitely.

HOW TO RE-CHECK THIS HEADER, IN ONE COMMAND

    rg -n 'AcpBetaFeatureFlag' crates/

At a5c4ca8b that yields three non-test call sites:
acp_thread/src/acp_thread.rs:8103 (the per-turn token count, item 1),
agent_servers/src/acp.rs:142 (the protocol choice, below) and
agent_servers/src/acp/v1.rs:671 (the v1 `subagents` client capability, see the
a5c4ca8b review below), plus the declaration, three imports, and
`cx.update_flags(..)` helpers inside `#[cfg(test)]` blocks. If that set changes, rewrite this header. If it empties out, or if every
remaining site gains an unconditional fallback, drop the patch from `PATCHES` in
the ebuild with a comment naming what superseded it.

Only the prose above was rewritten, in place, on 2026-07-31 (story 008,
sub-task 7.2). The hunks below are byte-identical to the original export, so the
`From` line still names the commit this patch was first exported from.


UPSTREAM TESTS THIS PATCH CHANGES (adapted 2026-10-09, corrected in 0.2.2_p20261009)

`enabled_for_all` wins over a settings override (crates/feature_flags/src/
store.rs, `try_flag_value`: "`enabled_for_all` always wins, including over user
overrides"), so with this patch acp-beta cannot be switched off. Two upstream
tests set it "off" and assert it is off before exercising code that does not
read the flag:

  agent  internal_tests::test_compact_prompt_routes_to_manual_compaction
  agent_servers  acp::tests::connection_routes_terminal_auth_without_acp_beta

Both keep upstream's "off" override and now assert the flag is ON. That is the
one setup where this patch decides the answer: a debug build counts as staff,
and `enabled_for_staff` defaults to true, so without the override the flag is on
with or without this patch. -r1 dropped the override, and its assertion passed
without the patch -- a test that proved nothing. Measured in 0.2.2_p20261009: with the
flags.rs hunk reverted, both fail. Their names are left as upstream has them.
This is the first change to the hunks themselves, so the "byte-identical"
sentence above holds for the flags.rs hunk only.

Refreshed onto 96984225 (0.4.0_p20261010): upstream stopped forcing acp-beta
"off" in test_compact_prompt_routes_to_manual_compaction and in the terminal-auth
routing test, and no longer gates terminal auth on the flag, so the two test
adaptations this patch carried are gone. What remains is the flag itself:
upstream still reads it in acp_thread for the per-turn token count.

Refreshed onto 92447a17 (0.6.1_p20261021): the flag now also picks the
protocol. Upstream fae8a35482 moved `connect` into agent_servers/src/acp.rs,
and with acp-beta on every ACP connection tries ACP v2 first and falls back to
v1 only when the agent refuses the version. Forcing the flag therefore makes
Zeo speak v2 to any agent that offers it. Claude Agent (Plus) answers protocol
1, so each Plus connection starts the adapter twice: the v2 attempt is
spawned, refused and dropped, and v1 spawns a fresh process -- a second
adapter process, and its startup time, per connection. 0057 makes both
attempts start a jailed command the same way. (The selection tests' flag-off
rows this paragraph once dropped are upstream's again since story 064, below.)

a5c4ca8b review (2026-10-25, story 061): acp-beta now gates a third thing. v1's
AcpConnection::stdio offers `clientCapabilities.subagents` only with the flag
(acp/v1.rs:671, through client_capabilities_for_agent), and derives
accepts_subagent_updates from it; without it, SubagentUpdate and SessionMessage
notifications are dropped with a warning. Forced on, every Zeo build advertises
the capability to every v1 agent and renders the subagent sessions they announce.
Claude Agent (Plus) emits no SubagentUpdate, so nothing changes for it; upstream's
claude-agent-acp does, and its subagents appear natively. The flag-off row of
upstream's stdio_offers_subagents_only_behind_acp_beta was dropped here for the
same reason, until story 064 made the forcing switchable (below).

a4b39c38 review: (2026-11-01, story 072) ACP moved from agent-client-protocol
3.5.0 (schema 1.12.0) to 3.6.0 (schema 1.13.0). The flag this patch forces is
unchanged: AcpBetaFeatureFlag is still NAME "acp-beta", a PresenceFlag, and its
uses in crates/ are the same set (15 at both bases). It still gates the
subagents capability: v1.rs:571 reads has_flag::<AcpBetaFeatureFlag>() into
offer_subagents, so the forcing keeps offering clientCapabilities.subagents to
every v1 agent, as the a5c4ca8b paragraph above describes.

SWITCHABLE OFF FROM SETTINGS (story 064, 2026-11-02)

The flag is still forced on, but the operator can now turn it off without a
rebuild: `"feature_flags": { "acp-beta": "off" }` in the settings reads off in
every build. Upstream honours that map only when `overrides_enabled()` (debug
or staff), and `enabled_for_all` wins over it even then, so before this the
forcing had no way back. FeatureFlagStore::try_flag_value now reads the
override for this one flag, OPERATOR_SWITCHABLE_FLAG = AcpBetaFeatureFlag::NAME,
ahead of `enabled_for_all`; resolved_key, which the settings UI shows, reads it
the same way so the UI shows the variant in effect. `overrides_enabled()` is
untouched and every other flag keeps upstream's gate. The flag is read when a
connection is made (agent_servers/src/acp.rs), so the switch applies to the
next connection; off, Zeo speaks ACP v1 and offers no `subagents` capability.

Because the flag can be off again, the test adaptations the paragraphs above
describe are gone: the selection tests and v1's subagents loop are upstream's
own flag-off rows again, so this patch no longer touches acp/v1.rs. Its tests:
feature_flags acp_beta_switch_tests.rs (on by default; off in a debug and a
non-staff process, ZED_DISABLE_STAFF=1; no other flag opened) and agent_servers
acp_beta_connect_tests.rs (the next connection takes the switch; v1 without
the subagents offer). The latter calls `connect` without a StdioLaunch, which
0057 adds to every caller, so it builds with USE=-ai-jail.

PLAN OPERATIONS RIDE THE FLAG TOO (Zed d2a4fb6d, 2026-11-08)

Upstream 1478fb5 (zed#66345, "acp: Support plan operations") added a second
unstable RFD behind the same flag: crates/agent_servers/src/acp/v1.rs:522
offers clientCapabilities.plan only `acp_beta.then(..)`. Forcing the flag on
therefore also offers plan operations to every v1 agent. Claude Agent (Plus)
still sends legacy `plan` updates and is unaffected; another ACP agent -- the
`claude-acp` entry, for one -- may now send its approved plan as markdown or
as a keyed plan, which Zed renders and never prunes. Turning the flag off in
the settings withdraws both offers at the next connection.
